#edb/ref 17592186101907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101072 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101072 #edb/ref 1045 "Optimization Problems in Economics" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101072 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186101902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101072 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101072 #edb/ref 1050 1500.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101072 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "0e211b6d-7f83-5173-9b92-9e29afdbbe93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101196 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101196 #edb/ref 1069 "q-15331" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101196 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101196 #edb/ref 1071 "A cinema hall can hold $120$ people. The price of each ticket is $$10.$ The cost of maintaining the cinema is made up of a fixed cost of $$400$ plus a cost of $$0.25$ per person. How many tickets should the cinema sell to maximize its profit?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101196 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "be45f13d-84b8-5eb7-9480-670af18ba1fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101196 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101196 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ tickets, the cinema has a revenue (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nR(x)=10\\cdot x=10x\n$$\n\nand a cost (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nC(x)={{400}_{⏟}}_{\\text{fixed cost}}\\,\\,+{{0.25x.}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variable cost}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =10x-(400+0.25x) \\\\  & =9.75x-400.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 120.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=120$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<120.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=9.75.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ has no solutions.\n\nOur candidates are $x=0$ and $x=120.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =9.75(0)-400=-400 \\\\ P(120) & =9.75(120)-400=770\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the cinema will have a maximum profit level when it sells $120$ tickets." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101902 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101902 #edb/ref 1061 644177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101902 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1fc21a4d-0b82-5f3e-b8d9-126d7179656f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101902 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186102014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101903 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101903 #edb/ref 1061 644178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101903 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "822eb919-1779-5cf4-9c9e-2c66f15e9e2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101903 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101904 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101904 #edb/ref 1061 644181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101904 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9f61e403-b029-5b57-97a2-0449bc4a4ffd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101904 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101905 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101905 #edb/ref 1061 644182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101905 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ab8f2b97-af90-5292-ad79-44e12e42010b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101905 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101906 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101906 #edb/ref 1061 644186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101906 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f5614397-09dd-526a-abea-3acccf854d69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101906 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101907 #edb/ref 1061 644190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101907 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ca0f41cd-a2e3-5d80-9d45-4b2c58377bc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101907 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101994 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the price function $p(x)$ gives the *price per unit* at which a company sells $x$ units of a good. \n\nThe revenue function $R(x)$ associated with a price function $p(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =(\\text{number of units})\\cdot (\\text{price per unit}) \\\\  & =x\\cdot p(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that the cost function $C(x)$ associated with producing $x$ units of a good can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(x) & =(\\text{fixed cost})+(\\text{number of units})\\cdot (\\text{cost per unit}) \\\\  & =(\\text{fixed cost})+x\\cdot (\\text{cost per unit})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe quantity $x\\cdot (\\textrm{cost per unit})$ is sometimes called the **variable cost** since it is the part of the cost function $C(x)$ that is dependent on the number of units $x$ produced by the company. \n\nFinally, if we know the revenue function $R(x)$ and the cost function $C(x),$ then the profit function $P(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nP(x) = R(x) - C(x).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101994 #edb/ref 1065 4663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101994 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fb6a794d-d60c-5d16-8912-b69af938c892" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101994 #edb/ref 1067 "A Review of Price and Cost Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102014 #edb/ref 1064 "There are several quantities that businesses need to keep track of to ensure that the business remains healthy:\n\n- Revenue: This is the total amount of money (in dollars) that a company gains after selling $x$ units of product.\n\n- Cost: This is the total amount of money (in dollars) that a company spends to create $x$ units of product.\n\n- Profit. The difference between revenue and cost. It represents the amount of *additional* money that the business has earned after paying its costs.\n\nBusinesses often strive to maximize revenue and profit. They also often seek to minimize cost. We can solve these problems using the second derivative or the candidates test. Let's find out how." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102014 #edb/ref 1065 682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102014 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7d29b308-bacd-5426-b6c8-43b0da8fcd75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102014 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537215 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186537999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537215 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186538019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537215 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186538028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537215 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186538047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537215 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186538052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537215 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537215 #edb/ref 1045 "Optimization Problems in Economics" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537215 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186537999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537215 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537215 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537215 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "9c2633a4-2f33-5904-a37a-770c0e5abd32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537267 #edb/ref 1080 "$$243\\,000$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537267 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537267 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "141d1a25-fba8-5358-bdf7-47d4083d0230" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537274 #edb/ref 1080 "188" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537274 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537274 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "16494579-7656-5c2d-a785-de9c67da7d88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537291 #edb/ref 1080 "15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537291 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 1759218